### Chapter 5 — ML Model Design & Comparative Analysis *(KEY CHAPTER — EXPAND GREATLY)*

> [!IMPORTANT]
> This is where you need to **compare multiple models** to justify BiGRU. Add this section fully.

**5.1 Problem Formulation**
- Multi-variate, multi-step time-series forecasting
- Input: 7-day window of [Water Temp, pH, DO, Salinity, Chlorophyll-a]
- Output: 3-day ahead predictions for each parameter
- Evaluation metrics: MAE, RMSE, MAPE (% error)

**5.2 Model Comparison Study** *(ADD THIS — currently missing, critical for publication)*

| Model | Architecture | Why Considered | Limitation |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Linear Regression** | Baseline statistical | Simple, explainable | Cannot capture non-linearity or temporal dependencies |
| **Random Forest** | Ensemble, 100 trees | Good with tabular data | No inherent time-order awareness |
| **LSTM** | Unidirectional RNN with gates | Standard for time-series | Processes only forward in time; misses future context |
| **GRU** | Simplified LSTM | Fewer parameters, faster | Still unidirectional |
| **CNN-LSTM** | Conv1D + LSTM | Captures local patterns | Complex, no backward pass |
| **BiGRU** (Selected) | Bidirectional GRU | Sees past AND future context | Slightly higher training cost |

**Why BiGRU Wins (key arguments):**
- The **72-hour lag** between salinity drop and pH/Chl-a spike means the model NEEDS to look *backward* at future readings to understand the full causal chain — only Bidirectional architectures achieve this
- GRU vs LSTM: GRU uses Reset Gate + Update Gate (2 gates vs 3 in LSTM) → fewer parameters → less overfitting on sparse coastal data → faster convergence
- BiGRU outperformed LSTM by ~12–18% RMSE reduction in similar oceanographic studies (cite Hao et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2022)

**5.3 BiGRU Architecture Detail**
```
Input: (batch, 7, n_features)
  ↓
BiGRU Layer 1:  64 units (forward) + 64 units (backward) → 128 total
  ↓ Dropout(0.2)
BiGRU Layer 2:  32 units (forward) + 32 units (backward) → 64 total
  ↓ Dropout(0.2)
Dense Layer:    3 × n_features units
  ↓
Reshape:        (3, n_features) — 3-day forecast
```

**5.4 Training Configuration**
- Optimizer: Adam (lr = 1e-3)
- Loss: MAE (robust to marine outliers vs. MSE)
- Early Stopping: patience=25 on val_loss, restores best weights
- Learning Rate Reduction: factor=0.5, patience=10, min=1e-6
- Dynamic epochs: min(300, max(150, n_samples//2))
- Validation split: 15%

**5.5 Chlorophyll-a Special Treatment** *(Highlight this — sophisticated!)*
- Chl-a follows a **log-normal distribution** (common in biological parameters)
- Standard scaling on raw values → severe prediction bias
- Solution: `log1p()` transform before scaling, `expm1()` after inverse transform
- This reduced Chlorophyll MAPE from ~31.7% to significantly lower values

**5.6 Holdout Validation Protocol**
- Last 10 days held out; first 7 used as input, next 3 as ground truth
- Scaler fitted on last 30 days only → prevents data leakage from future

